<section class="nc-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Reading the default deal</h2>
    <p>The calculator opens with a $3,000,000 property earning $30,000 a month in rent plus $1,500 in other income. Here is how those inputs become the numbers investors and lenders look at:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Line</th><th class="num">Annual</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Scheduled income ($31,500 × 12)</td><td class="num">$378,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Less 5% vacancy and credit loss</td><td class="num">−$18,900</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Effective gross income</td><td class="num">$359,100</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Operating expenses, including 5% management</td><td class="num">−$117,955</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Net operating income (NOI)</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>$241,145</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Debt service on a $2.1M loan, 6.75%, 25-year amortization</td><td class="num">−$174,110</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Capital reserve</td><td class="num">−$12,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pre-tax cash flow</td><td class="num">$55,035</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>From those lines: the going-in cap rate is 8.04% (NOI ÷ price), debt service coverage is 1.39× (NOI ÷ debt service), debt yield is 11.5% (NOI ÷ loan), and cash-on-cash return is 5.2% on the $1,060,000 of cash invested (down payment, closing costs, and initial repairs). Expenses run 32.8% of effective gross income.</p>
    <p>Notice the gap between the 8.04% cap rate and the 5.2% cash-on-cash return. At a 6.75% interest rate with 25-year amortization, the loan constant is about 8.3%, higher than the property's cap rate, so each borrowed dollar costs more than it earns. That is negative leverage: debt is lowering the first-year cash return rather than raising it. Principal paydown and growth may still make the deal work, which is what the hold projection tests.</p>

    <h2>How sensitive value is to the cap rate</h2>
    <p>Value under direct capitalization is NOI ÷ cap rate, so small cap rate changes move value a lot. At the default NOI of $241,145:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Cap rate</th><th class="num">Implied value</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>6.5%</td><td class="num">$3,709,923</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7.0%</td><td class="num">$3,444,929</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7.5%</td><td class="num">$3,215,267</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8.0%</td><td class="num">$3,014,312</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Each half point is worth $200,000 to $265,000 here. That is why the exit cap rate is one of the most important assumptions in the model, and why conservative underwriting usually sets it higher than the going-in cap rate, as the default does (7.5% vs. 7.0% target).</p>

    <h2>How lenders read the same numbers</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>DSCR.</strong> Commercial lenders commonly require at least 1.20× to 1.25×, and more for riskier property types. At 1.39×, the default deal has room for NOI to fall about 13% before it reaches 1.20×.</li>
      <li><strong>Debt yield</strong> shows how much income the property produces per dollar of loan, regardless of interest rate or amortization. Lenders use it to size loans when rates are low enough to flatter DSCR.</li>
      <li><strong>Loan maturity.</strong> A 25-year amortization with a 10-year term leaves a large balance due at year 10. The deal has to support a refinance at whatever rates exist then.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Underwrite actuals, not the brochure</h2>
    <p>Sellers' pro formas often show full occupancy, market rents, and thin expenses. Build the APOD from the trailing 12 months of actual income and expenses and the current rent roll, then adjust for known changes: property tax reassessment after the sale (often the biggest surprise), insurance renewals, leases rolling over, and deferred maintenance. Enter that stabilized version here and compare it with the seller's figures.</p>
  </div>
</section>
